BT /F1 12 Tf 14 TL 72 720 Td () Tj T* (THE WATER CYCLE) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Water on Earth is always moving. It travels between the oceans, the air, and the) Tj T* (land in a loop called the water cycle. The water that falls as rain today may) Tj T* (have been in a river last week, in a cloud yesterday, or in an ocean long before) Tj T* (that. No water is made and none is lost. The same water is used again and again,) Tj T* (powered by heat from the sun and pulled down by gravity.) Tj T* () Tj T* (This reading follows one drop of water around the loop. It explains each step in) Tj T* (plain words, gives an everyday example, and finishes with why the cycle matters) Tj T* (to the people, plants, and animals that depend on it.) Tj T* () Tj T* (WHERE WATER IS FOUND) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Most of Earth's water, about 97 percent, is salt water in the oceans. Only about) Tj T* (3 percent is fresh water, and most of that is locked away as ice in glaciers and) Tj T* (at the poles. The small amount left is in lakes, rivers, soil, underground, and) Tj T* (in the air. Even though the share in the air is tiny, it is the part that makes) Tj T* (weather and brings rain, so it is very important.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Water exists in three forms. As a liquid it flows, as in a river or a glass of) Tj T* (water. As a solid it is ice, snow, or hail. As a gas it is called water vapor,) Tj T* (which is invisible and mixes with the air. The water cycle is mostly the story) Tj T* (of water changing between these three forms.) Tj T* () Tj T* (EVAPORATION) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (The sun warms the water in oceans, lakes, and rivers. As the water heats up, its) Tj T* (tiny particles move faster. Some of them move so fast that they escape from the) Tj T* (surface and rise into the air as a gas called water vapor. This change from) Tj T* (liquid to gas is called evaporation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Evaporation happens faster when it is hot, dry, or windy. A shallow puddle on a) Tj T* (sunny afternoon can disappear in an hour, while the same puddle on a cool, damp) Tj T* (day may stay for days. The oceans supply most of the water vapor in the air,) Tj T* (because they cover most of the planet.) Tj T* () Tj T* (An everyday example is wet laundry on a clothesline. The clothes dry because the) Tj T* (water in them evaporates into the air. Another is the steam that rises from a) Tj T* (cup of hot tea. The steam you can see is already turning back into tiny drops,) Tj T* (but the invisible vapor right above the cup came from evaporation.) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
endstream
