BT /F1 12 Tf 14 TL 72 720 Td (fall. Any water that falls from clouds to the ground is called precipitation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (What falls depends on the temperature of the air. When it is warm enough all the) Tj T* (way down, the drops fall as rain. When the air is below freezing, the water) Tj T* (freezes into ice crystals and falls as snow. Sleet forms when raindrops freeze) Tj T* (as they fall through a layer of cold air. Hail forms inside strong storm clouds,) Tj T* (where wind carries drops up and down through freezing air, and each trip adds) Tj T* (another layer of ice until the stone is heavy enough to fall.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The amount of precipitation is very different from place to place. Some deserts) Tj T* (get less than 25 millimeters of rain in a year, while some tropical mountains) Tj T* (receive more than 10 meters.) Tj T* () Tj T* (COLLECTION) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Water that falls does not disappear. Some of it runs over the surface of the) Tj T* (land and gathers in streams, which join to form rivers, which flow to lakes and) Tj T* (finally to the ocean. This water is called runoff. Some of the water soaks into) Tj T* (the ground. It moves slowly through soil and rock and is stored underground as) Tj T* (groundwater. In many places people reach groundwater by digging wells.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Some of the precipitation falls as snow in cold places and stays frozen for) Tj T* (months or even thousands of years in glaciers and ice sheets. When the snow) Tj T* (melts in spring, it feeds rivers with fresh water. Much of the water that people) Tj T* (drink comes from rivers, lakes, and groundwater that began as precipitation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Plants take up some of the water that soaks in. The sun warms the water in the) Tj T* (oceans, lakes, and rivers again, and more of it evaporates. The cycle begins) Tj T* (once more. It has no start and no end.) Tj T* () Tj T* (PUTTING IT TOGETHER) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Here is the whole loop in order. The sun heats water, and some of it evaporates.) Tj T* (Plants add to this when they transpire. The vapor rises and cools, and condenses) Tj T* (into clouds. The drops in the clouds grow heavy and fall as precipitation. The) Tj T* (water is collected in rivers, lakes, oceans, and underground, and the cycle) Tj T* (repeats.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The time one drop spends in each place varies enormously. A drop may stay in the) Tj T* (atmosphere for only about nine days, in a river for a couple of weeks, in a lake) Tj T* (for years, and deep in the ocean or in an ice sheet for thousands of years.) Tj T* () Tj T* (WHY THE WATER CYCLE MATTERS) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
endstream
