BT /F1 12 Tf 14 TL 72 720 Td () Tj T* (The water cycle moves fresh water to the land. Without it, rain would not fall) Tj T* (on farms and forests, and rivers would dry up. It also spreads heat around the) Tj T* (planet. When water evaporates it takes heat from the surface, and when it) Tj T* (condenses high in the sky it gives that heat back to the air, which helps drive) Tj T* (winds and storms.) Tj T* () Tj T* (People can affect the cycle. Cities cover soil with roads and roofs, so rain) Tj T* (runs off quickly instead of soaking in. Cutting forests reduces transpiration,) Tj T* (which can mean less rain nearby. Pollution on the land can be carried by runoff) Tj T* (into rivers and groundwater. Protecting wetlands, planting trees, and using) Tj T* (water carefully all help the cycle keep working for everyone.) Tj T* () Tj T* (KEY TERMS) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Water cycle: the continuous movement of water between the oceans, the air, and) Tj T* (the land.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Evaporation: liquid water changing into a gas called water vapor.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Transpiration: water leaving a plant through its leaves as vapor.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Condensation: water vapor cooling and changing into liquid droplets.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Precipitation: water falling from clouds to the ground as rain, snow, sleet, or) Tj T* (hail.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Collection: fallen water gathering in rivers, lakes, oceans, and underground.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Runoff: water that flows over the land into streams and rivers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Groundwater: water that has soaked into the soil and rock underground.) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
endstream
